You can filter the child `NodeList` by `instanceof Element`. Here's a Java 8 version:

```java
import org.w3c.dom.Element;
import org.w3c.dom.Node;
import org.w3c.dom.NodeList;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;

public static List<Element> getChildElements(Node samlAssertionNode) {
    List<Element> elements = new ArrayList<>();
    NodeList children = samlAssertionNode.getChildNodes();
    for (int i = 0; i < children.getLength(); i++) {
        Node child = children.item(i);
        if (child.getNodeType() == Node.ELEMENT_NODE) {  // or: child instanceof Element
            elements.add((Element) child);
        }
    }
    return elements;
}
```

Usage:

```java
List<Element> childElements = getChildElements(SAMLAssertionNode);
```

`getChildNodes()` also returns text, comment, and whitespace nodes, so the type check is needed. If you want a streaming version:

```java
import java.util.stream.Collectors;
import java.util.stream.IntStream;

List<Element> childElements = IntStream.range(0, children.getLength())
        .mapToObj(children::item)
        .filter(n -> n.getNodeType() == Node.ELEMENT_NODE)
        .map(Element.class::cast)
        .collect(Collectors.toList());
```

If you only need elements with a particular name, such as `saml:Subject` or `saml:Conditions`, use `getElementsByTagNameNS(namespaceURI, localName)` on the parent. It returns only matching descendants, not just direct children, so filter by `getParentNode()` if you need direct children only.

